# Start a Jido Instance

This tutorial starts a Jido instance from a script, moves it into a supervision tree, and runs an Agent inside it. A Jido instance is the named supervisor that owns your running Agents.

Actions and Flows run through `Jido.Exec.run/4` without an instance. You need one when an Agent runs as a process.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "migration/consolidated-v3"}
])
```

## Define an Agent to run

This is the invoice Agent from [Getting Started](getting-started.livemd), with an inline Action to keep the tutorial short.

```elixir
defmodule StartInstance.InvoiceAgent do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "start_instance_invoice"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{paid_cents: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/billing/invoice"

    route "invoice.payment.apply", as: :apply_payment do
      action %{amount_cents: amount},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{amount_cents: Jido.Joi.integer()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | paid_cents: context.agent_state.paid_cents + amount}}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

## Start the default instance

`Jido.start/1` suits scripts and Livebook. With no `:name`, it starts the default instance, whose name is the module-style atom Jido.Default. Calling it again returns the running instance's PID and ignores any new options.

```elixir
{:ok, default_pid} = Jido.start()
{:ok, ^default_pid} = Jido.start(max_tasks: 2_000)

Process.whereis(Jido.Default) == default_pid
```

The cell returns `true`.

Pass the instance name to every instance function.

```elixir
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(Jido.Default, StartInstance.InvoiceAgent, id: "inv-1001")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(server)

{Jido.agent_count(Jido.Default), Jido.whereis_agent(Jido.Default, "inv-1001") == server}
```

The cell returns `{1, true}`.

The Agent links to the instance's supervisor, not to the process that started it. Stopping the instance stops its Agents.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop()

{Process.whereis(Jido.Default), Process.alive?(server)}
```

The cell returns `{nil, false}`.

## Define an instance module

An application defines a module with `use Jido`. `:otp_app` is required, and it names the application whose config the module reads.

```elixir
defmodule StartInstance.Jido do
  use Jido,
    otp_app: :start_instance,
    namespace: "start-instance/primary"
end
```

The `:namespace` option is a nonempty string that gives this instance a stable identity. You need it for stable Agent Refs and for persistent Agents. One namespace can bind to one live instance per node.

## Add the instance to a supervision tree

In an application, put the module in the children list in `lib/my_app/application.ex`. This cell starts the same tree directly so that you can see it run.

```elixir
children = [StartInstance.Jido]

{:ok, tree} = Supervisor.start_link(children, strategy: :one_for_one)

Supervisor.which_children(tree) |> Enum.map(&elem(&1, 0))
```

The cell returns `[StartInstance.Jido]`. The instance module's name is the instance's name.

```elixir
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(StartInstance.Jido, StartInstance.InvoiceAgent, id: "inv-2001")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(server)

signal = StartInstance.InvoiceAgent.apply_payment_signal!(%{amount_cents: 700})
{:ok, %Jido.AgentServer.TurnResult{status: :committed}} = Jido.AgentServer.call(server, signal)

Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(server).agent.state
```

The cell returns `%{paid_cents: 700}`.

## Configure the instance

The module reads `config :my_app, MyApp.Jido` and merges it with any options in the child spec. `config/0` shows the result. This cell uses `Application.put_env/3` in place of a config file.

```elixir
Application.put_env(:start_instance, StartInstance.Jido, max_tasks: 2_000)

StartInstance.Jido.config()
```

The cell returns `[max_tasks: 2000]`. Jido reads this config when the instance starts, so it applies to the next start.

| Option | Meaning | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:name` | The instance name, a non-nil atom. Required by `Jido.start_link/1`. A `use Jido` module uses its own name. | The default instance under `Jido.start/1` |
| `:namespace` | Stable identity for Refs and persistent Agents. | `nil` |
| `:max_tasks` | Child limit for the instance's Task supervisor, a nonnegative integer or `:infinity`. | `1_000` |
| `:persistence` | Adapter, as a module or `{module, options}`, for `Jido.thaw/4`, `Jido.activate_agent/3`, and `persistence: :inherit`. `Jido.start_agent/3` ignores it unless you pass `persistence: :inherit`. | `nil` |
| `:agent_server` | Default AgentServer runtime options for this instance. | `[]` |
| `:debug` | Log detail for this instance: `false`, `true`, or `:verbose`. | `nil` |

An unknown option returns an error.

```elixir
{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{details: %{keys: keys}}} =
  Jido.start_link(name: StartInstance.Other, max_agents: 10)

keys
```

The cell returns `[:max_agents]`.

## Run two instances

Two instances have separate registries, so the same Agent ID can run in each. Use this to keep tenants or test runs apart.

```elixir
{:ok, _second} = Jido.start(name: StartInstance.Second)
{:ok, other_server} = Jido.start_agent(StartInstance.Second, StartInstance.InvoiceAgent, id: "inv-2001")

{
  other_server != server,
  Jido.whereis_agent(StartInstance.Jido, "inv-2001") == server,
  Jido.whereis_agent(StartInstance.Second, "inv-2001") == other_server
}
```

The cell returns `{true, true, true}`.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop(StartInstance.Second)
:ok = Supervisor.stop(tree)

Process.whereis(StartInstance.Jido)
```

The cell returns `nil`.

